INTRODUCTION :

Splunk: An Introduction
Splunk is a data platform that turns machine-generated data into actionable insights. Originally known for log management,
it has evolved into a comprehensive observability, security, and analytics platform used by enterprises worldwide.


What It Does
At its core, Splunk ingests, indexes, and searches machine data — logs, metrics, traces, events from servers, applications, 
networks, sensors, and more. It transforms raw, unstructured data into structured intelligence you can query, visualize, and alert on.


Key Capabilities :

| Area                           | What It Covers                                                                            |
| ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Search & Investigation**     | SPL (Splunk Processing Language) lets you query data with powerful, SQL-like syntax.      |
| **Monitoring & Observability** | Real-time dashboards, infrastructure health, application performance (APM).               |
| **Security (SIEM/SOAR)**       | Threat detection, incident response, compliance reporting via Splunk Enterprise Security. |
| **IT Operations**              | Root cause analysis, anomaly detection, predictive maintenance.                           |
| **Visualization**              | Custom dashboards, reports, and alerts.                                                   |


Core Architecture

[ Data Sources ] → [ Forwarders ] → [ Indexers ] → [ Search Heads ] → [ Users/Dashboards ]

Forwarders — Lightweight agents that collect and ship data
Indexers — Store and index incoming data for fast retrieval
Search Heads — Handle user queries and distribute them across indexers
Deployment Server — Manages configuration across the fleet


